+The separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\mathcal P/\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\times D,B\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. Complete scalar multiplication for all $t\in\mathbb R$ by $0[(A,B)]:=[(1,1)]$ and, for $t<0$, $t[(A,B)]:=[((-t)B,(-t)A)]$; here $-t>0$, so only the already specified positive scaling of types is used. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.
